<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Delagoa Bay</h1><p class="st-meta">1721 CE – 1730 CE · capital Fort Lijdzaamheid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Dutch East India Company trading post on Delagoa Bay, founded to seek gold and ivory and abandoned after a decade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1721 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>) sent an expedition from the Cape to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>, hoping to tap the gold of the interior and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> of the bay. The settlers built Fort Lijdzaamheid on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The post traded cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and metal for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves with the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsonga-people">Tsonga</a> rulers, including those of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tembe-kingdom">Tembe Kingdom</a>. It suffered terribly from malaria, losing much of its garrison, and was attacked and briefly captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> in 1722. Expeditions inland failed to find the hoped-for goldfields.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Unprofitable and deadly, the post was abandoned in 1730. The Portuguese reasserted their claim to the bay, which was later contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> before the 1875 arbitration confirmed Portuguese possession.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1721 CE – 1730 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.95%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1721 CE – 1730 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Lijdzaamheid</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fort Lijdzaamheid</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=dutch-delagoa-bay" aria-label="Dutch Delagoa Bay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5541 3132 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Delagoa Bay lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5861 3388L5097 634M5861 3388L5469 3663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0M5469 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5861 3388h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5861 3388h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fort Lijdzaamheid. <a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=dutch-delagoa-bay">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

